			<p class="date">[ 2026-08-16 ]</p>
			<h1>Drilling as an Investment in Information</h1>

			<p>An investor is buying exposure to the asset’s future value, the entity that owns that asset is using the sourced money to change that value, a drilling program is one mechanism by which they can do that. A development-stage asset undertaking a resource definition drill program is an example of that owner attempting to reduce uncertainty in a mineral resource and effectively better define the resource (Laplace: “Probability is relative, in part to this ignorance, in part to our knowledge”). A drill metre does not change the geology but instead what is known about the Geology. That information may create, destroy, or have no impact on the asset’s value, but the program’s value may be in providing evidence that might weaken, invalidate or fail to support assumptions underlying the mine plan</p>

			<h2>Targeting Decision-Relevant Uncertainty</h2>
			<p>Uncertainty-reducing data can come from a variety of program types aiming to improve confidence in a specific variable, it is in the interest of the investor/owner to ensure that their capital is being used to target the specific confidence variable (ie. Density, Grade, Metallurgy, Geotech Conditions) that most impacts a downstream economic outcome (ie. Mine Life) and in turn impacts the next decision made. This process must consider whether a specific program planned on the asset is likely to resolve that uncertainty, and if the potential results could influence that decision, this must be weighed against the cost of obtaining that data.</p>

			<h2>Technical Assessment Across the Investment Lifecycle</h2>
			<p>This process that an investor goes through to assess how its capital is being deployed through a drill program involves technical assessment, this process involves asking three key questions, of course the purpose of these questions varies depending on whether the assessment is being conducted pre-investment (Technical Due Diligence), or post-investment (Portfolio Support).</p>
			<ul>
				<li>How will results from a resource definition program influence a decision based on the downstream economic outcome it affects?</li>
				<li>How do we assess if a drill program that is planned on an asset will resolve uncertainty?</li>
				<li>What costs are involved in obtaining this data?</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>How will results from a resource definition program influence a decision based on the downstream economic outcome it affects?</h2>
			<p>My experience is in RC and Diamond drilling programs in a Tier 1 mining company. Whilst Resource Definition should be considered as principally reducing uncertainty in grade, tonnage, geology and continuity, Geotechnical and Metallurgical programs form an essential part of the wider definition programme from an investor’s perspective as they reduce uncertainty that can materially alter mine design, recovery, cost, mine life and hence project value. Below I map out some examples of how specific programs map to potential consequences, downstream economic outcomes, and finally possible decisions based on the consequence and potential outcome.</p>

			<div class="table-wrap">
