			<h2>How do we assess if a drill program that is planned on an asset will resolve uncertainty?</h2>
			<p>Collecting data alone does not necessarily reduce decision-relevant uncertainty, the data collected must instead test an assumption, and change confidence in that assumption. A basic example would be that we are building a mine plan based on the assumption that the proposed slope geometry will remain acceptably stable under specified geological, structural and groundwater conditions. Data collected to update our confidence in this assumption would include structural mapping (spacing, surface roughness, orientation), measuring groundwater levels, pore pressure, and data required to test dewatering response.</p>
			<p>Knowing if datasets will reduce uncertainty in an assumption is hard, this is where storing physical data assets is valuable as if more data is needed to test an assumption later, the asset can be referred to again, it effectively provides valuable optionality (though is restricted to only some potential datasets). It also extends further than just choosing what data to select, but also requires establishment of location, quantity, resolution and data quality.</p>
			<p>This process here is effectively designing a test, in line with the scientific method this involves translating the underlying assumption into testable hypotheses (discretise assumption into a variety of variables), then whether a proposed sampling design is representative (Drilling a Geotechnical diamond hole that does not cover the relevant wall sector or depth would not be representative). The conditions that would cause a change in the underlying assumption must then be defined prior to testing. Finally, uncertainty in measurements and the model must also be accounted for.</p>

			<h2>What costs are involved in obtaining this data?</h2>
			<p>Geoscientific data is multimodal and only valuable when it is interpreted effectively. There is a complex and often overlooked series of contributing factors to obtaining geoscientific data. The most obvious cost is acquisition, what sensor is needed, who collects that data. Validation and QAQC of datasets is a hugely important step, and can correlate with significant processing charges and time delays. Integration of data is concerned with using verified data within the context of other datasets by leveraging consistent locations, intervals, units of measurement and geological domains. Time must then be spent interpreting datasets and determining what the acquired information is actually saying, finally the data must be presented to decision makers in a sufficient manner, these steps require deep technical expertise.</p>
			<p>There are also other hidden costs:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Operational disruption and enabling costs of obtaining a dataset are the knock-on effects of extracting the data, ie. Rig downtime, additional personnel on site.</li>
				<li>Opportunity costs: These are related to what data is not being collected due to resource allocation to a specific dataset. A 6-Inch Diamond Metallurgical program is a good example, conducting ore-processing tests on these samples restricts the ability to utilise the extracted asset for other further testing.</li>
				<li>Finally, I see a need to model the inherent risk of obtaining data. This is very broad but can include the risk presented by placing staff in remote areas, or the downstream impacts of losing a radioactive downhole-geophysics tool in a drillhole. Of course, many of these variables are constraints as opposed to tradeable costs, in the case of safety these risks must be reduced to an acceptable level (also incurs cost), only then can an economic model account for exposure arising from residual risk (ie. Remote road accident).</li>
			</ul>
			<p>It is also essential to utilise as much pre-existing (but reliable and verified) data as possible, the workflow should continually seek decision-relevant uncertainty reduction relative to total cost, time and risk. It is also important to incorporate newly acquired data into the drill program, effectively ensuring that the programme is capable of adapting at specified decision points. There is room to consider where drillholes can be used for multiple purposes, and a broad view of what downstream decisions need to be made can help align drillholes to optimise data usage.</p>
			<p>There is a lot of work in the industry currently to convert historical data into standardised, quality-rated and decision-usable datasets, without obscuring uncertainty about its reliability (KoBold’s work with SMART Zambia and the Zambian Geological Survey). This presents a wealth of opportunity, my work has largely been focussed around streamlining data workflows and ensuring data undergoes effective QAQC. Where I see this going is using AI to streamline the process by mapping inconsistent legacy logging terminology onto standardised vocabularies and flagging anomalous values for geological review. Finally, if certain data bottlenecks such as lab processing time can be resolved (potentially using on-rig/near-rig sample analytical systems) and data latency reduced, we will be in a great place to make drill programs more dynamic. Faster decision-ready data allows low-value holes to be identified, stopped, redirected and remaining metres allocated against the latest geological interpretation.</p>

			<h2>Turning Assumptions into Testable Hypotheses</h2>
			<p>It is important to note that any material, uncertain assumptions made during due diligence should be expressed as hypotheses and should become testable after investment.</p>

			<h3>Pad and Access Readiness</h3>
			<p>The most common assumption I have seen internally made when planning a drill program is that the pads and access track for the drill program will be ready for the rig on time. This assumption could be tested through portfolio monitoring utilising spatial data, and earthwork asset telemetry (i.e. GuidEx) reconciled against authoritative pad completion dates to conduct ongoing evaluation of the earthworks schedule.</p>

			<blockquote>
				<h3>From Portfolio Monitoring to Portfolio Support</h3>
				<p>Findings from this monitoring process must then materialise as actual portfolio support by quantifying the deviation, analysing its cause, and implementing a response (an example of a response may be to balance the dozer-to-excavator ratio). Of course the extent to which an investor or portfolio-support team does this depends on its governance rights, but I would argue that assessing such assumptions is essential, and perhaps monitoring of key material assumptions warrants establishment in some cases during the transaction stage.</p>
			</blockquote>

			<h3>Updating Penetration-Rate Assumptions</h3>
			<p>Similarly, an allocated program-wide penetration rate is often assigned based on broad averages spanning long timeframes across many projects, it is essential to monitor this pen rate throughout a drill program and test any assumptions that contributed to the initially applied figure. An outcome from monitoring this on active drill programs is to test whether improved specificity in assumptions made when calculating the pen rate improves forecasting.</p>
			<blockquote>“Do rig and core-diameter-specific estimates predict future penetration rates more accurately than the programme-wide average?”</blockquote>
			<p>A broad assumption may be sufficient to approve an initial budget. The logic must be Bayesian; portfolio monitoring should progressively combine prior evidence with new evidence relating to the rig, geology and operating conditions encountered.</p>

			<h3>Reflecting Operational Evidence in an Investment Case</h3>
			<p>Operational evidence must eventually feed back into the investment case. Changes in pad readiness and penetration rates affect programme cost, schedule and the timing at which decision-ready evidence becomes available. Monitoring should therefore update the programme forecast and economic case, allowing remaining capital to be continued, redirected or withheld.</p>

			<h2>Conclusion</h2>
			<p>An investor is not ultimately underwriting drill metres, but the information those metres are expected to produce and the decisions that information supports. Due diligence should identify the material assumptions within an investment case, whilst portfolio monitoring should test and update those assumptions as evidence becomes available. A drill programme creates value when it supports a better decision, whether that decision is to commit further capital, redesign the programme or avoid a larger loss.</p>
